<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dagomba people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dagbamba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dagomba, or Dagbamba, are a people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> who speak Dagbani, a Gur language. Their kingdom, Dagbon, was founded by mounted invaders probably in the 15th century and is ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ya-na">Ya Na</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>. Many Dagomba are Muslims, and the kingdom was an important trading power on the routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa states</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dagomba-people.jpg" alt="Dagomba people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dnshitobu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tibung_cultural_display_activities_18.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Dagomba live in Dagbon, a homeland of about 20,000 km2 in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>, and their population is over 3.1 million, with communities also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. They speak Dagbani, which belongs to the Mabia (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mole-dagbani">Mole-Dagbani</a>) subgroup of the Gur languages. Before the kingdom took shape, the territories of Dagbon were decentralised and headed by the Tindaamba, the aboriginal chiefs, who are chosen by an oracle and still lead traditional religious acts.</p><p>According to tradition, the ruling class descends from Naa Gbewaa, whose ancestors migrated from the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a>. Gbewaa unified the Dagomba into a kingdom, and his descendants, the Nanima, centralised it. The Dagomba are historically related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, whose kingdoms tradition says were founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a>, a daughter of Naa Gbewaa. The Dagomba are regarded as senior to the Mossi states of Ouagadougou, Yatenga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fada-ngourma">Fada N'Gourma</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> and Dagomba states eventually ruled the lands of the entire northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volta-river">Volta</a> basin. One account holds that their second period of success ended with the restoration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> power towards the close of the 15th century. The kingdom also held administrative responsibility for groups that had no chiefs of their own, among them the Konkomba, Bimoba, Chamba and Wala.</p><p>In the late 17th century King Zanjina became the first ruler to practise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Islam has been the state religion since his time. His reign saw economic growth and the founding of learning centres, and a school known as the University of Moliyili was established in 1700. Dagbani was written in a modified Arabic script. The Dagomba resisted European colonisation even after German forces burnt down and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>, and Dagbon was never subjugated until it was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Chieftaincy is the main feature of Dagomba society. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ya-na">Ya Na</a>, or paramount king, stands at the head of a tiered system of territorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, and chiefs traditionally sit on a stack of skins. The king's seat is a collection of lion and cow skins, so the political system is often called the Yendi Skin. Oral history is kept by drummers who act as professional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, known as Lunsi.</p><p>The Dagomba are among the few African peoples to reserve royal titles for women: female chiefs such as the Gundo Naa and the Kpatu Naa hold lands and rule over male subjects. Inheritance is largely patrilineal, though that of certain Tindaamba is matrilineal. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, many follow the Dagbon Traditional Religion, with its supreme god Naawuni and local shrines. Festivals include Damba, the Yam Festival and the Bugum or Fire Festival, which both Muslims and non-Muslims celebrate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dagomba people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
